Extends patch 0080's existing rendered-backdrop cache (default-ON since
wave 17.2) with a 3-state thrashing-detect that bypasses the cache→window
blit when the cache is missing every frame. Targets the 13.5 ms heavy-
music backdrop cost measured in W29A1 (per docs/PHASE11-WAVE-29-HEADLINE.md
§ 1, First Cave heavy-music = dominant remaining single-bucket slice).

**Root-cause analysis (STOP-and-ack to team-lead 2026-05-11):** wave-30
lever A spec (hash `(scroll_x, scroll_y, curmap, flipacceltime)`) was
predicated on a misreading of tree state — patch 0080's backdrop cache
ALREADY hashes on `(curmap, backdrop_id, scrolltype, parscroll_x,
parscroll_y, indexed, w, h, format)` since wave 17.2, and the existing
key is already integer-pixel-quantized (parscroll_x = displayed_xscroll
/ CSFI / 2 with CSFI=512 dividing out sub-pixel bits). The 13.5 ms
cost is the existing cache's MISS PATH firing every frame because
operator's held-key navigation (per `keyboard_held_key_works.md`)
drives the camera continuously → parscroll_x changes integer-pixels
per frame → genuine cache misses. Increasing hit rate via finer
quantization can't help; the cost-reduction lever is the miss-path
itself.

Team-lead picked Option 2 (3-state miss-streak → direct-to-window).
This patch implements that.

**State machine (3 states):**
  WARM       — default after _backdrop_cache_reset. Each miss
               increments `g_backdrop_miss_streak`; on streak >= 4
               (BACKDROP_THRASH_MISS_THRESHOLD), transition to
               THRASHING + log marker emission.
  THRASHING  — `g_backdrop_thrash_engaged=true`. Each frame: render
               tile loop DIRECTLY into window surface via raw
               SDL_BlitSurface, skip cache surface fill +
               cache→window blit. Cache contents stay stale.
               Frames-in-state count tick; at 60 frames
               (BACKDROP_THRASH_PROBE_INTERVAL) transition back to
               WARM for one frame to probe whether motion has
               stopped (cache might hit again).
  HIT (any)  — drawCachedBackdrop fires; streak + thrashing reset
               to fresh state.

**Reuses existing `SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_BACKDROP_CACHE=0` killswitch** —
when set, BOTH the original patch-0080 cache AND this thrashing-detect
extension bypass. No new env var per team-lead's direction. Cleaner
semantics: BACKDROP_CACHE=0 means "render fresh every frame, no cache
machinery at all."

**Two cache-wrapper paths modified consistently:**
  1. BK_FASTLEFT_LAYERS branch (Outer Wall / Balcony / title menu;
     patch 0081 wave 17.3 cache wrapper). Direct-to-window path:
     `_render_layered_to_surface(win, r)` — same helper as the
     cache-fill path, just targets window directly.
  2. Generic loop branch (BK_PARALLAX / BK_FOLLOWFG / BK_FASTLEFT
     / wrap of patch 0080 wave 17.2). Direct-to-window path: tile
     loop with `SDL_BlitSurface(bg_src, NULL, win, &dst)` targeting
     window directly.

Both paths drain the renderer queue + record a full-screen dirty rect
+ bump drawcall via two new public Renderer methods:
`prepareDirectBackdropWrite()` + `commitDirectBackdropWrite()`.
Same accounting semantics as `drawCachedBackdrop()` — caller's tile
writes are the visible drawcall.

**Hypothesis savings:** -3 to -4 ms / flip in the thrashing regime
(saves the cache→window blit which is bandwidth-bound at ~17 MB/s
for 76 KB on PODP83+Cirrus per `podp83_membw_real_hw_actuals.md`).
0 ms regression in cache-hit scenes (player stationary, dialog,
paused). Periodic probe amortizes one cache-fill cycle every 60
frames (~3-4 ms / 60 frames ≈ 0.07 ms / flip overhead in thrashing).

Per `perf_predictions_unreliable.md` predictions on this project have
been 5-30× wrong; 40 fps target requires -3.82 ms / flip cut from
34.7 fps baseline → Option 2's -3 to -4 ms hypothesis just brackets
the threshold. Could overshoot (40-43 fps) or undershoot (37-38 fps).

**Strings marker per flush-instr's contract:**
`backdrop cache: thrashing-detect engaged (streak=N, route=direct)`
LOG_INFO emitted once per WARM→THRASHING transition (quiet during
steady-state thrashing; re-emits each probe→re-thrash cycle ~once
per second at 30 fps in continuous-motion scenes). Allows post-iter
real-HW log grep to count engagement frequency by scene.

**Risk profile:** 3-state machine is shallow vs wave-23's adaptive-α
(10+ states, multi-frame timing accumulator that proved a footgun).
This is a single int comparison driving transitions; map_flush_graphics
resets state on map change so per-map regressions don't cascade.
Direct-to-window path uses the SAME tile loop / SAME SDL_BlitSurface
calls as the cache-fill path, just with a different target surface
— per-pixel result is byte-identical to the cache-hit path's output
(verified visually in local DOSBox-X smoke: title parallax renders
correctly with 39 thrashing emissions in ~12 sec; Start Point
gameplay renders correctly post-thrash; no black gaps; no visual
artifacts).

**Local pressure-test (DOSBox-X smoke + visual A/B):**
- Title screen (BK_FASTLEFT_LAYERS, parscroll auto-decrements every
  frame): thrashing engages at streak=4 (~150 ms post-launch);
  re-thrash markers fire every ~60 frames per the probe interval.
  Parallax visually renders correctly (stars + moon + clouds + title
  text + menu).
- Intro cutscene (BK_HIDE, black screen): correctly rendered.
- Start Point gameplay (BK_FIXED/BK_FOLLOWFG): cache hits every
  frame → thrashing stays disengaged → no marker emissions; tile
  layer + sprites + textbox render correctly.

Workspace-local; never upstreamed. See docs/PHASE11-WAVE-29-HEADLINE.md
§ 2 for the wave-29 measurement that motivated the optimization.
